    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> founded Antioch in 300 BCE, and it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and then of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where it may have had over 500,000 inhabitants at its peak.</li><li>It was a chief centre of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the place where the name Christian is said in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> to have first emerged, and the seat of one of the five original patriarchates.</li><li>Earthquakes, Persian sacks in 256 and 540, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> wars and a change in trade routes reduced it, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> took it in 1268 it never recovered its size.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1432 there were only about 300 inhabited houses within the walls of a city that had held, at its peak, perhaps over 500,000 people.</p></aside>
    </section>

