    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> founds Antioch</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">240 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a>, Antioch becomes the main centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">64 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Antioch passes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">115</span><span class="rf-text">A huge earthquake strikes while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> is in the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">540</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> destroys Antioch and deports its people to Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">969</span><span class="rf-text">Byzantine forces capture the city after a siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1098</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> take Antioch after a siege of eight months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1268</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captures and razes the city</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alalakh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alalakh.jpg" alt="Alalakh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_of_Alalakh_(Tell_Atchana).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fkitselis, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Antioch stood in the southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a>, a fertile lowland watered by three rivers, between the slopes of Mount Silpius and the left bank of the Orontes, at an elevation of 90 metres. The plain connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a> and the deserts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and it had been inhabited for thousands of years before the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> sites there included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Tell Atchana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-tayinat">Tell Tayinat</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered the region in the mid-sixth century BCE, and it remained under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> invaded in 333 BCE.</p><p>The writers Libanius and John Malalas connected the site with Greek legends of earlier settlements, among them Iopolis, Kasiotis and Herakleia, and Libanius held that Alexander had meant to found a city there. There is no proof for any of these claims. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> I at Ipsus in 301 BCE he ordered the foundation of four settlements between 301 and 299 BCE: Seleucia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieria">Pieria</a> and Laodicea on the coast, Antioch and Apamea inland. According to Malalas and Libanius an eagle carried sacrificial meat to the future site of Antioch, showing the place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> favoured. Scholars now hold that the city was named for Seleucus' father Antiochus, not for his son of the same name.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Antioch&#8221; cites where it covers Early history, Foundation and naming:</p><ul><li>Glanville Downey, A History of Antioch in Syria: From Seleucus to the Arab Conquest (Princeton University Press, 2015)</li><li>Andrea U. De Giorgi, Antioch: A History (Routledge, 2021)</li><li>Kutlu Aslihan Yener (ed.), Surveys in the Plain of Antioch and Orontes Delta, Turkey, 1995–2002 (Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus.jpg" alt="Seleucus II Callinicus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus">Seleucus II Callinicus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Seleucus_II_Callinicus_(cropped),_Antioch_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The original city was laid out in imitation of the grid plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> by the architect Xenarius. The citadel stood on Mount Silpius, and two great colonnaded streets crossed in the centre. Antiochus I Soter added a second walled area, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus">Seleucus II Callinicus</a> a third on an island in the Orontes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> a fourth, after which Antioch was known as the Tetrapolis. According to ancient tradition 5,500 Athenians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonians-ethnic-group">Macedonians</a> were settled there with an unknown number of native Syrians, and the free Greek settlers with their families probably numbered between 17,000 and 25,000. Its importance dates chiefly from the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a> in 240 BCE.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the city grew larger still. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> visited in 47 BCE, a Roman forum was laid out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> built colonnades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> paved the great east to west street with granite, and baths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> were built, the finest aqueduct by Hadrian. The hippodrome measured more than 490 metres in length and could hold up to 80,000 spectators. Antioch may have had over 500,000 inhabitants at its peak, making it the third largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> after Rome and Alexandria, and its walls, which first enclosed about 90 hectares, may have enclosed 500 hectares by 540 CE. Most of the surviving Antioch mosaics date from the fourth and fifth centuries, its golden age.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator.jpg" alt="Seleucus I Nicator" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seleukos_I_Nikator_Bronze_Roman_100BCE-100CE_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_Naples_AN_5590_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Gluck, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> founded the city, and it was the capital of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> under Antiochus I and the Seleucid capital from 240 BCE until 63 BCE. The people took sides in the quarrels of the Seleucid house and rose in rebellion against Alexander Balas in 147 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II Nicator</a> in 129 BCE. They invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> to occupy the city in 83 BCE and petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against the restoration of Antiochus XIII in 64 BCE. Antioch then passed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, though it remained a civitas libera, and it became the capital of Roman Syria. From the early 4th century it was the seat of the comes Orientis, head of the Diocese of the East.</p><p>After the Persian sack of 540 and Persian conquest in 613 the city was taken in 637 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ubayda-ibn-al-jarrah">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. Byzantine forces recaptured it in 969 after a siege, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a> took it in 1084. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> conquered it in June 1098 and it became the centre of the Principality of Antioch, ruled by Bohemond, Tancred, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a> and their successors, several times under regents and at times as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Byzantine emperors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> took it in 1268. It entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1516 as a sub-province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and from 1831 to 1840 it was the headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom.jpg" alt="John Chrysostom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnchrysostom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Antioch's early population was a mix of local settlers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonians-ethnic-group">Macedonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, who were given full status from the beginning. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was the main centre of Hellenistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, and Jews lived in a quarter called the Kerateion. Outside the city lay the park of Daphne, about 8 kilometres south, with its springs and its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>; its beauty and lax morals were celebrated across the ancient world. The city was known for its fertile plain, which produced wheat, barley, olives, oil and wine.</p><p>Antioch was a chief centre of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> asserts that the name Christian first emerged there, and according to tradition Saint Peter was among the first missionaries. Ten assemblies of the church were held there between 252 and 300, and Constantine began the Great Church in 327. A school of Christian thought, led by Diodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and Theodore of Mopsuestia, took its name from the city. In 362 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> implied that pagans and Christians lived quite harmoniously, and the sophist Libanius and the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a> lived there. In the Arab period Christians, Muslims and Jews lived together, with periods of tension, and Christians remained the dominant population until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="Trajan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Glyptothek_Munich_72.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Antioch lay on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> Rift and suffered more than sixty notable earthquakes, around ten of them above magnitude 7. John Malalas recorded one in 148 BCE that did immense damage. Others struck in 37, in 115, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> took shelter in the circus and the population fell to less than 400,000, in 526 and 528, and in 588, when the Domus Aureus of Constantine was destroyed. The Orontes and the streams from Silpius also flooded the city regularly, and the alluvium came to bury it metres deep.</p><p>In 256 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> raided the city and many people were slain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The city was burned, some 100,000 inhabitants were killed and the rest deported to Gundeshapur, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> recaptured it the next year. In 540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> completed the destruction begun by the earthquakes, deporting the people to a new city in Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and Antioch lost as many as 300,000 people. Anti-Jewish riots broke out in 490 or 491, in which the Green faction attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>; the accounts differ on the details. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> took the city on 18 May 1268 and razed it, killing or enslaving nearly the entire population. In 1355 it still had a considerable population, but by 1432 only about 300 inhabited houses stood within its walls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Antioch was called the cradle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> because of its longevity and the role it played in the emergence of early Christianity. It became the seat of one of the five original patriarchates, and five churches still use the title of patriarch of Antioch for their prime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> Orthodox Church, the Maronite, Syriac Catholic and Melkite Greek Catholic Churches, and the Greek Orthodox Church of Antioch. Most have moved their seats to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and the Maronite Church to Bkerké in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, which continues the Antiochene liturgical tradition. The Tyche of Antioch, a seated figure crowned with the city's walls and holding wheat stalks, with the Orontes swimming under her feet, became its civic symbol.</p><p>Little of the Roman city can be seen. The remains lie mostly beneath alluvial deposits from the Orontes, and what shows are the fortification walls on the mountains east of the modern city, several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and the Church of St Peter, said to have been a meeting place of an early Christian community. Excavations between 1932 and 1939 did not find Constantine's church or the imperial palace, but recovered nearly 300 mosaics from villas and baths in Antioch, Daphne and Seleucia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieria">Pieria</a>. Many are in the Hatay Archaeology Museum in Antakya, and others hang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> Art Museum and the museums of the other sponsoring institutions.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Antioch was founded in 300 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> on the Orontes, in what is now Antakya in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It was a Seleucid and Roman capital and an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and earthquakes, Persian sacks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> wars reduced it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> razed it in 1268.</em></p>

